.TH NEMURI 1 "October 2026" "nemuri 2.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nemuri \- terminal player for ASMR and music
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nemuri
.RI [ DIRECTORY ]
.br
.B nemuri
.BI --set-library " PATH"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nemuri
opens a local music library in a terminal interface. With no argument it uses
the configured library, or the XDG Music directory on first use. A positional
directory overrides the library for the current session only.
.PP
Scanning and fuzzy search run in the background. Metadata is cached in SQLite;
settings and named playlists are stored separately according to the XDG base
directory specification.
.PP
The player is aimed at long recordings such as ASMR, organised one folder per
creator, and plays ordinary music as well. Tracks are listed by their file names
as stored on disk, without the extension and without using tag titles; a renamed
file shows its new name after the next scan. The second line of each entry shows
the top-level folder under the library root, exactly as named (files directly
in the root share a root label), followed by the artist, album, and format when
known.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --set-library " PATH"
Save PATH as the main music library and start the player.
.TP
.BR -h , " --help"
Show command-line help.
.TP
.BR -V , " --version"
Show the program version.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.BR Up , Down , j , k
Select a track.
.TP
.B Enter
Play the selected track immediately without clearing the manual queue.
.TP
.B Space
Pause or resume playback.
.TP
.BR Left , Right , h , l
Seek backward or forward by 10 seconds.
.TP
.BR Shift+Left , Shift+Right , H , L
Seek backward or forward by 60 seconds.
.TP
.BR 0 \(en 9
Jump to 0 through 90 percent of the current track.
.TP
.B b
Add a jump mark at the current position of the playing or paused recording.
Only recordings of 10 minutes or more take marks, and a mark within one second
of an existing one is refused. The progress bar shows marks as |, and when the
current section's mark has a name, the now playing border shows it.
.TP
.B B
Open the jump marks of the playing recording: Enter jumps to the selected mark,
e renames it (an empty name leaves only its time), d deletes it, and Esc or B
closes the panel.
.TP
.BR { , }
Jump to the previous or next jump mark. Within 3 seconds after a mark,
.B {
goes to the one before it, and before the first mark it goes to the start;
after the last mark,
.B }
stays put.
.TP
.BR - , =
Lower or raise volume by 5 percent.
.TP
.BR [ , ]
Lower or raise volume by 1 percent.
.TP
.B y
Show or hide lyrics. Lyrics come from an LRC file next to the audio file with
the same name, or from lyrics embedded in its tags. Synchronized lyrics are
preferred over plain text, and the LRC file over embedded lyrics. Wide
terminals keep a lyrics pane beside the library, even for tracks without
lyrics: synchronized lyrics scroll with the current line highlighted, and plain
lyrics scroll with playback progress. Narrower terminals show the current
synchronized line on the now playing border. Hiding lyrics gives the library
the full width. LRC files may be UTF-8, GBK, or UTF-16
with a byte order mark. Placeholder text that music platforms put in place of
lyrics, such as "Instrumental" or its Chinese equivalents, is treated as no
lyrics, and a placeholder LRC file falls back to the embedded lyrics. The
choice is restored on the next launch.
.TP
.B m
Toggle mute.
.TP
.BR , " or " .
Move the left/right balance 10 steps to the left or right, between -100 and
100. Leaning to one side only lowers the other side, which is silent at the
end of the range. Mono files are balanced as well. The setting applies at once
and is restored on the next launch.
.TP
.B w
Swap the left and right channels, for headphones worn the wrong way round. The
balance refers to the ears and applies after the swap. The now playing bar
shows the balance and the swap whenever they are not at the default. The choice
is restored on the next launch.
.TP
.B M
Enable or disable mouse support. With the mouse enabled, a single click selects
a row, a double click plays it, the wheel moves the selection, and clicking the
progress bar seeks. Mouse capture takes over the terminal's own drag selection;
most terminals restore it while Shift is held. The choice is restored on the
next launch.
.TP
.B i
Toggle privacy mode. While it is on, the system media controls (MPRIS on Linux,
SMTC on Windows) receive only a generic title instead of the track title,
artist, album, and file path, so desktop and lock-screen widgets cannot show
what is playing; playback can still be controlled from them. The now playing
bar marks the mode. Off by default; the choice is restored on the next launch.
.TP
.BR n , p
Play the next track or return through actual playback history.
.TP
.B z
Cycle repeat among stop-after-track, sequential, repeat-all, and repeat-one.
Stop-after-track, the default, stops when a track ends on its own; n still
moves on by hand and queued tracks still play. Media controls report it as no
loop.
.TP
.B s
Toggle shuffle. When enabled, the library is played as a reshuffled permutation each round.
.TP
.B t
Cycle the sleep timer among off, 15, 30, 60, 90, and 120 minutes, and the end
of the current recording. The countdown is shown first in the now playing bar.
A minute timer runs on wall-clock time, also while paused; over its last 30
seconds the output fades out, then playback pauses and keeps its position. The
end-of-recording timer fades out the last 30 seconds of whatever is playing and
stops when it ends, regardless of the repeat mode and the queue. The fade never
changes the volume setting; pressing t again restarts the countdown and restores
full volume. The fade length is set in the
.B sleep
block of the configuration.
.TP
.B v
Show or hide the audio spectrum. The choice is restored on the next launch.
.TP
.BR o , O
Cycle the library sort field among path, title, artist, album, and duration, or
toggle between ascending and descending. Sequential playback follows the list
order. Missing values always sort last. The choice is restored on the next
launch.
.TP
.B /
Enter live fuzzy search over file names, relative paths, and tag titles,
artists, and albums.
.TP
.B f
Open the folder panel, which lists every top-level folder of the library with
its track count. Enter shows only the selected folder; the first row shows all
tracks again. While a folder is chosen, search, sequential playback, repeat,
and shuffle stay inside it. The choice is restored the next time the same
library is opened, unless the folder is gone.
.TP
.B r
Rescan the library in the background.
.TP
.BR a , A
Append the selected track to the queue or make it the next track.
.TP
.B Q
Open the play queue panel. Inside it, Enter jumps to an entry and discards the
entries before it, d removes one entry, J and K move the selected entry, and c
clears the queue.
.TP
.B P
Open the named playlist panel.
.TP
.B ?
Open the complete in-application help. On short terminals it scrolls with the
arrow keys, j and k, or the mouse wheel.
.TP
.B q
Quit.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings live in
.IR config.kdl ,
written in KDL v2. The player creates it with comments on first start,
carrying over the settings of a pre-1.4.0 config.toml, which is left in
place.
.PP
.RS
.nf
library "~/Music"
playback {
    volume 80
    muted #false
    repeat "stop"       // stop, none, all, or one
    shuffle #false
    balance 0           // -100 (left only) to 100 (right only)
    swap-channels #false
}
sort "artist" descending=#false
interface {
    visualizer #true
    lyrics #true
    mouse #true
    privacy #false
}
sleep {
    fade 30             // seconds, 0 to 600; 0 means no fade
}
theme {
    preset "light"
    selection-bg "#C8D3F0"
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Booleans are #true and #false, and strings are double-quoted. A library path
may start with ~ for the home directory. Sort keys are path, title, artist,
album, and duration. Changes take effect on the next start.
.PP
On exit the player re-reads the file and rewrites only the settings changed
while it ran (volume, mute, repeat, shuffle, balance, channel swap, sort, and
the visualizer, lyrics, mouse, and privacy switches), in place, keeping comments and layout; settings missing
from the file are appended to their block. Other edits made while it runs are
kept.
.B --set-library
rewrites only the library line. The sleep fade is read at start and never
written back.
.PP
A wrong value or unknown key is ignored on its own and reported with its line
number in the footer at start-up. A syntax error is reported with its line and
column; the player then uses the defaults and leaves the file untouched.
.PP
The
.B theme
block picks a preset and may override single colors. The presets are
.B default
(grey-white levels for dark terminals),
.B light
(dark greys for light terminals), and
.B terminal
(palette colors only, following the terminal's own scheme). The colors that can
be overridden are primary, muted, border, selection-bg, danger, spectrum-low,
and spectrum-high. A color is "#RRGGBB", a name such as "red" or "dark-gray", a
palette index from "0" to "255", or "reset" for the terminal's default
foreground. An unknown preset falls back to default and an unreadable color
keeps the preset value. The player never paints a background of its own.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/nemuri/config.kdl
Library, playback, sort, interface, and theme settings; see
.BR CONFIGURATION .
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/nemuri/playlists/*.json
Independent named playlist files.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/nemuri/session.toml
Track, position, and folder filter at the last exit. On the next launch with
the same library, the folder filter is restored and that track is loaded paused
at the saved position; press Space to continue. Deleting the file only forgets
these.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/nemuri/progress.json
Listening progress of recordings of 10 minutes or more. Starting such a
recording continues where it was left unless it was finished; 0 jumps back to
the start. Library rows show the progress, and a renamed file keeps it after
the next scan. An unreadable file is renamed aside and a new one is started.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/nemuri/marks.json
Jump marks of recordings of 10 minutes or more, written as soon as they change.
Marks follow a renamed file after the next scan, and marks past the end of a
shortened file are kept but not shown. An unreadable file is renamed aside and
a new one is started.
.TP
.I $XDG_CACHE_HOME/nemuri/library.sqlite3
Rebuildable incremental music metadata cache.
.PP
Version 1.4.0 and earlier were called Music Player, with the command
.B music-player
and the folders
.IR tui-music-player .
When a
.I nemuri
config or data folder does not exist yet, the first start copies the old
folder there and leaves the old one untouched, so an older version still
works; the cache is rebuilt instead. If the copy fails, the player does not
start and names both folders.
